    part st058 : Statement {
        :>> id = "84e823cb-e70b-4a4f-b38e-08f675ec2f2a";
        :>> title = "GH#14 An unrecognized --flag silently becomes the ROOT argument - keel guard --read gated a phantom directory";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-08-31"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5";
        :>> text = "keel 0.2.3 (build 1546d19). `keel guard --read .` is parsed with '--read' as ROOT: the guard suite runs against a nonexistent directory named '--read', producing 0-scanned rows, absence warnings (declared rule not present), and exit 1 - while plain `keel guard` on the real tree exits 0.\n\n**Cost in the field:** a session read the phantom run's FAILED as a broken warning-read-ledger, concluded commits were hard-blocked for the whole team, escalated to peers, and told the human - all downstream of one silently-swallowed flag. A second session lost `guard --help` to the same trap.\n\n**Suggested control:** refuse any argument starting with '-' that is not a recognized flag, naming it ('unknown flag --read; ROOT must be a directory') - never bind it as a path. A cheap invariant at every subcommand's arg parse.\n\nTwo-session repro: run `keel guard --read .` on any project; compare with `keel guard .`.";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-08-24"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/sysmlv2-ai-toolkit/issues/14";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part st059 : Statement {
        :>> id = "7b4e60b2-2b2b-4303-819d-6e94a450e248";
        :>> title = "GH#9 keel land can report 'landed' while just-authored facts never reach the pushed commit";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-08-31"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5";
        :>> text = "Found in Penumbra (recorded there as issue125), keel 0.2.3+.\n\n**What happened:** a session authored three Claim records via hand-edit of .tracking/claims/<actor>.sysml, ran `keel land`, and got 'keel land: landed.' Three days later, git ancestry showed those additions first reached a commit that day - the claims never existed in any pushed tree, so the exclusion they assert was phantom for three days. guard:claim-ancestry caught it at the eventual commit (good control), but only then.\n\n**Root:** on a shared checkout with branch churn, land can push while the working tree's just-authored facts are uncommitted - and still print 'landed'.\n\n**Suggested control:** land should either (a) commit-then-push what the write API just authored, or (b) refuse with 'authored but uncommitted: <files>' - never print 'landed' over a working-tree-only write. A cheap invariant: after land, `git status --porcelain .tracking/` must be empty or land names what it left behind.";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-08-24"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/sysmlv2-ai-toolkit/issues/9";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

